# Southeast Asia batch (1511-2021): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"as15117": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese commander Afonso de Albuquerque set out to seize Malacca, the port that controlled the strait between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea, and with it the spice trade. He stormed the city in August 1511, the sultan fled, and Portugal built the fortress of A Famosa. Portugal held the chokepoint for 130 years; Muslim traders moved to rival ports, and the strait passed to the Dutch in 1641 and the British in 1824.",
 now="The Strait of Malacca carries a large share of world trade and most of China's oil imports, a vulnerability Chinese strategists call the 'Malacca dilemma'.",
 story="Malacca, a sultanate on the strait between Sumatra and Malaya, controlled the shipping lanes between India and China and was the port where Indian, Arab, Chinese and Malay merchants met. Portuguese commander Afonso de Albuquerque stormed the city in August 1511. The sultan fled. Portugal built a fortress, A Famosa, to hold the harbour.",
 why="Holding Malacca meant taxing the spice trade from the Moluccas. The port was the main meeting place of the Indian Ocean and China Sea trades.",
 then="Muslim merchants moved to rival ports such as Aceh, Johor and Banten, which spread trade and Islam across the archipelago. Portugal taxed the strait without ever controlling the region's commerce. The Dutch took Malacca in 1641 and the British in 1824.",
 fact="The gate of A Famosa, the Porta de Santiago, still stands in Malacca, one of the oldest European structures in Southeast Asia."),
"as157112": dict(
 bluf="Spanish commander Miguel López de Legazpi set out to give Spain a base near China and the Spice Islands. In 1571 he took the Muslim port of Maynila on Luzon from its ruler, Rajah Sulayman, and made it the capital of the Spanish Philippines, where Chinese traders met the silver arriving from Mexico. Spain ruled the islands for 327 years and made the Philippines Asia's leading Catholic nation.",
 now="About 80 per cent of Filipinos are Catholic. Spanish names and loanwords, and the separate identity of the Muslim south, show where Spanish rule reached and where it stopped.",
 story="Spanish commander Miguel López de Legazpi captured the Muslim port of Maynila on Luzon from its ruler, Rajah Sulayman, in 1571. He made it the capital of the Spanish Philippines. Its harbour linked China's traders with the silver arriving from Mexico.",
 why="Spain wanted a base near China and the Spice Islands. Manila had an excellent harbour.",
 then="Manila became the hub of the galleon trade between Acapulco and Asia. Chinese merchants settled there in large numbers and were periodically expelled or massacred. Spain ruled the islands for 327 years, until 1898.",
 fact="The Manila galleon sailed between Manila and Acapulco for 250 years, from 1565 to 1815."),
"se-batavia": dict(
 bluf="VOC Governor-General Jan Pieterszoon Coen wanted a secure headquarters from which the Dutch East India Company could control the spice trade and keep the English out. He captured Jayakarta in May 1619, razed it and built Batavia. From there the company enforced its monopolies by force and took Java piece by piece, and the territorial state it built became the Dutch East Indies and then Indonesia.",
 now="Jakarta grew from Batavia, and Indonesia's borders follow those of the Dutch East Indies that were ruled from here, a common pattern in which colonial administrative units became nation-states.",
 story="VOC Governor-General Jan Pieterszoon Coen captured the town of Jayakarta in May 1619, razed it and built Batavia as the company's headquarters. He enforced the spice monopoly by force. In 1621 his troops killed, enslaved or drove out most of the people of the Banda Islands.",
 why="The VOC needed a secure base to control the spice trade and keep the English out.",
 then="From Batavia the VOC enforced its spice monopolies and took control of Java through wars and alliances with local rulers. The city's segregated colonial society included a large Chinese community, which Dutch troops and townsmen massacred in 1740.",
 fact="Coen's reported maxim was that there could be no trade without war and no war without trade."),
"d-banda": dict(
 bluf="VOC Governor-General Jan Pieterszoon Coen set out to give the Dutch East India Company a monopoly of nutmeg and mace, which grew only on the Banda Islands. In 1621 his troops killed, starved, enslaved or drove out most of the islands' perhaps 15,000 people and divided the land into plantations worked by slaves. The company held the nutmeg monopoly for over a century, and company rule by force became its model across the archipelago.",
 now="Banda set the pattern of a trading company governing by force, the model the VOC and later the British East India Company applied for two centuries; it is now at the centre of Dutch debates about the colonial past.",
 story="Nutmeg and mace grew only on the Banda Islands, whose people sold them to the English despite Dutch demands for exclusive rights. In 1621 VOC Governor-General Jan Pieterszoon Coen invaded. Of perhaps 15,000 Bandanese, most were killed, starved or enslaved, and a few fled. The islands were divided into nutmeg plantations worked by enslaved labourers.",
 why="Nutmeg and mace grew only on Banda. The Bandanese kept selling them to the English despite Dutch demands for exclusive rights.",
 then="The VOC held the nutmeg monopoly for over a century, until the French and British smuggled seedlings to their own colonies. Its model of company rule by force spread across the archipelago.",
 fact="In 1667 the Dutch kept Run, the one Banda island the English had held, and England kept the Dutch colony of New Amsterdam, today's Manhattan."),
"d-malacca1641": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch East India Company and its ally the Sultanate of Johor set out to take Malacca from Portugal and with it the strait between the Indian Ocean and China. They besieged the city from August 1640 and stormed it on 14 January 1641 after famine and disease had killed much of the population. Portuguese power in Southeast Asia ended, the VOC became the region's dominant European force, and Malacca declined as the Dutch routed trade through Batavia.",
 now="The Strait of Malacca is still one of the world's busiest shipping chokepoints, carrying much of the trade and oil between the Indian Ocean and East Asia, and who controls it remains a strategic question.",
 story="The VOC, allied with the Sultanate of Johor, besieged Portuguese Malacca from August 1640. Famine and disease killed much of the population. Dutch troops stormed the city on 14 January 1641.",
 why="Control of the strait meant control of trade between the Indian Ocean and China. Johor's sultans descended from the rulers Portugal had driven out in 1511 and wanted the Portuguese gone.",
 then="The VOC centred its trade on Batavia, and Malacca became a secondary port. Britain took it in 1824.",
 fact="The Dutch held Malacca for 183 years and built the Stadthuys of 1650, among the oldest Dutch buildings in Asia."),
"se-ayutthaya": dict(
 bluf="Burma's Konbaung kings set out to crush Siam, their old rival. After a 14-month siege their armies stormed Ayutthaya in April 1767, burned the 400-year-old capital and carried off tens of thousands of captives. The Siamese state was refounded within a year at Thonburi and then at Bangkok under a new dynasty, and Burma was fixed as Siam's enemy for two centuries.",
 now="The fall of Ayutthaya destroyed the Siamese state and forced its refounding at Bangkok under a new dynasty that still reigns; the memory fixed Burma as Siam's enemy for two centuries.",
 story="Ayutthaya had been Siam's capital for 400 years and one of Asia's great trading cities. The armies of Burmese King Hsinbyushin besieged it for 14 months. In April 1767 they stormed the city, burned it and carried off tens of thousands of captives.",
 why="The Konbaung kings wanted to crush their old rival. Ayutthaya's wealth made it a prize.",
 then="General Taksin drove the Burmese out within a year and founded a new capital at Thonburi. In 1782 General Chakri took the throne as Rama I and moved the capital across the river to Bangkok.",
 fact="Ayutthaya's ruins are a World Heritage site."),
"se-chakri": dict(
 bluf="General Chakri took the Siamese throne as King Rama I to rebuild the kingdom after Ayutthaya's fall on a more defensible site. He built a new capital at Bangkok on the east bank of the Chao Phraya, modelled on Ayutthaya, and extended Siam's control over parts of Laos, Cambodia and Malaya. Siam became the strongest power of mainland Southeast Asia, and the dynasty he founded still reigns.",
 now="Thailand's king is still a Chakri, and the monarchy remains politically central, protected by strict lèse-majesté laws.",
 story="King Taksin, who had ruled Siam from Thonburi since the fall of Ayutthaya, was deposed and executed in 1782. His leading commander, General Chakri, took the throne as King Rama I. He built a new capital on the east bank of the Chao Phraya river at Bangkok, modelled on Ayutthaya.",
 why="The kingdom needed rebuilding at a more defensible site. The east bank put the river between the capital and Burma.",
 then="Siam became the strongest power in mainland Southeast Asia, extending control over parts of Laos, Cambodia and Malaya.",
 fact="Bangkok's full ceremonial name, which begins Krung Thep Maha Nakhon, is one of the longest place names in the world."),
"as181926": dict(
 bluf="East India Company official Stamford Raffles wanted a British base at the southern end of the Strait of Malacca to counter Dutch control of the region's trade and secure the route to China. In 1819 he signed a treaty with a Johor prince and the local Malay chief for a trading post and made it a free port with no import duties. Singapore grew into the British Empire's key Asian port and the base of one of the world's richest city-states.",
 now="Singapore is now one of the world's richest countries per person and among its busiest ports; its founding as a free port explains its enduring openness.",
 story="In 1819 East India Company official Stamford Raffles signed a treaty with a Johor prince and the local Malay chief to set up a trading post on Singapore island. He made it a free port with no import duties. Traders and migrants arrived at once, and the settlement grew rapidly.",
 why="Britain wanted to counter Dutch control of the region's trade and secure a base on the route to China. A free port drew the traders the Dutch ports taxed.",
 then="Singapore grew into the British Empire's key Asian port, the hub for trade between India, China and the Malay world, and a magnet for Chinese immigrants. The Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 confirmed British possession.",
 fact="Raffles founded the Zoological Society of London in 1826, the year he died."),
"se-burma1824": dict(
 bluf="The East India Company went to war to push Burma's Konbaung armies back from the border of British Bengal, where they had arrived after conquering Arakan, Manipur and Assam. The Company won in 1826 at a cost of 15,000 soldiers, most of them killed by disease, and took Assam and Burma's coastal provinces. The war began a 60-year conquest completed in two more wars, and it set the colonial border between Burma and India.",
 now="The border drawn after this war, between Arakan and Bengal, runs through areas where questions of belonging still drive conflict, including the Rohingya crisis.",
 story="Burma's Konbaung kings had conquered Arakan, Manipur and Assam, bringing their armies up to the border of British Bengal. Border clashes in Assam and Bengal led to war in 1824. The East India Company landed an army at Rangoon. Disease killed most of the 15,000 Company soldiers who died. Burma lost Assam and its coastal provinces by the Treaty of Yandabo in 1826.",
 why="Burma's expansion had brought it to the border of British Bengal. The Company would tolerate no rival power on that frontier.",
 then="Two more wars, in 1852 and 1885, completed the British conquest. Burma was ruled as a province of British India until 1937.",
 fact="The Company's paddle steamer Diana, which towed boats up the Irrawaddy, was the first steamship used in a war in Asia."),
"se-javawar": dict(
 bluf="Javanese Prince Diponegoro rose against Dutch interference in Java's courts, land and taxes, claiming a religious mission as a just ruler. Five years of war killed about 200,000 Javanese and 15,000 colonial soldiers before he was captured at peace talks in 1830 and exiled. The cost pushed the bankrupt Dutch to the Cultivation System, and Diponegoro became a hero of Indonesian independence.",
 now="The war's cost drove the Dutch to the Cultivation System of 1830 and to direct rule of Java; Diponegoro's revolt is the founding story of Indonesian resistance to Dutch rule.",
 story="Javanese Prince Diponegoro, an Islamic mystic, rebelled in 1825 after the Dutch built a road across his family's land. The war killed about 200,000 Javanese and 15,000 colonial soldiers. Diponegoro was captured at peace talks in 1830 and exiled.",
 why="Dutch interference in Javanese courts, land and taxes came first. Resentment of tolls and tax farmers gave the revolt its following. Diponegoro's claim to be a just ruler with a religious mission gave it a leader.",
 trig="The Dutch built a road across Diponegoro's family land in 1825.",
 then="The war's cost pushed the bankrupt Dutch to introduce the Cultivation System in 1830. Diponegoro died in exile in Makassar in 1855.",
 fact="Javanese painter Raden Saleh's 1857 picture of Diponegoro's arrest, painted as an answer to a Dutch version of the scene, is a national icon in Indonesia."),
"se-cultivation": dict(
 bluf="Dutch Governor-General Johannes van den Bosch set out to make Java pay for a Netherlands left near bankruptcy by the Java War and Belgium's breakaway. From 1830 villages had to set aside a share of their land, often a fifth, to grow sugar, coffee or indigo for the state at fixed prices. Java became the most profitable colony in the world and paid for Dutch debts and railways, at the cost of famine in parts of Java in the 1840s, and its exposure gave Indonesian nationalism its first grievance.",
 now="The system made Java the most profitable colony in the world and paid for the Dutch state's debts and railways, at the cost of Javanese famine; its exposure forced a reform of colonial policy and gave Indonesian nationalism its first grievance.",
 story="Dutch Governor-General Johannes van den Bosch introduced the Cultivation System in 1830. Villages had to set aside a share of their land, often a fifth, to grow sugar, coffee or indigo for the government at fixed prices. Profits paid off Dutch debts and built Dutch railways. Famine hit some regions of Java in the 1840s.",
 why="The Netherlands was nearly bankrupt after the Java War and Belgium's breakaway in 1830.",
 then="The Dutch novel 'Max Havelaar' of 1860 exposed the system's abuses. The system was wound down from 1870 in favour of private plantations.",
 fact="'Max Havelaar', the 1860 novel that exposed it, gave its name to a fair-trade label."),
"se-bowring": dict(
 bluf="Siamese King Mongkut set out to keep Siam out of a war with Britain after watching Britain defeat China and Burma. He accepted free trade, low tariffs and extraterritorial rights for British subjects on terms brought by British envoy Sir John Bowring in 1855. Siam opened to Western commerce, signed similar treaties with other powers and began to modernise, and it kept its independence while every neighbour was colonised.",
 now="The treaty set the pattern by which Siam traded concessions for survival, from 1855 to the border cessions of 1893 to 1909; Thailand is the only Southeast Asian country never colonised by a European power.",
 story="Siamese King Mongkut (Rama IV) had watched Britain defeat China in the Opium War and Burma in two wars. British envoy Sir John Bowring arrived in Bangkok in 1855. Mongkut agreed to low tariffs, free trade and extraterritorial rights for British subjects.",
 why="Mongkut chose trade concessions rather than giving Britain a pretext for war.",
 then="Siam signed similar treaties with the United States and France in 1856 and with other Western powers after. Rice exports grew and Siam began to modernise.",
 fact="Mongkut spent 27 years as a Buddhist monk before taking the throne and taught himself English and Latin."),
"se-chulalongkorn": dict(
 bluf="Siamese King Chulalongkorn set out to make Siam strong and 'civilised' enough that no colonial power would have an excuse to take it. Over a reign of 42 years he abolished slavery, built railways, schools and a modern civil service, and ceded border territories to France and Britain. Siam became a centralised nation-state and survived as a buffer between British Burma and French Indochina, the only country in the region never colonised.",
 now="Chulalongkorn's centralised state, built between 1868 and 1910, is the Thai state of today, and his reputation as the king who kept Siam independent underpins the monarchy's prestige.",
 story="Siamese King Chulalongkorn (Rama V) reigned from 1868 to 1910. He abolished slavery and the prostration of subjects before the king, and built railways, schools and a modern civil service. He gave up Laos and western Cambodia to France and the northern Malay states to Britain to keep the core of Siam free.",
 why="Britain held Burma and Malaya and France held Indochina. A state with modern administration and fixed borders gave neither a pretext.",
 then="Siam stayed independent as a buffer between British Burma and French Indochina. It gave up Laos to France in 1893, western Cambodia in 1907 and four northern Malay states to Britain in 1909.",
 fact="Chulalongkorn was tutored as a boy by Anna Leonowens, the governess of 'The King and I'."),
"as188739": dict(
 bluf="France's Third Republic set out to build an Asian empire for markets, prestige against Britain and a route into southern China. It conquered southern Vietnam from 1858, made Cambodia a protectorate in 1863, took northern and central Vietnam after a war with China in 1883-85 and combined them into the Indochinese Union in 1887, adding Laos in 1893. The colony's borders made modern Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia, and resistance to it produced Vietnamese nationalism, communism and three decades of war.",
 now="French Indochina's borders created modern Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia, and the struggle against French rule led directly into the Vietnam wars.",
 story="France conquered southern Vietnam from 1858 and made Cambodia a protectorate in 1863. It took northern and central Vietnam after a war with China in 1883-85. In 1887 it combined them into the Indochinese Union; Laos was added in 1893. The Vietnamese emperors at Hue kept their thrones under French residents.",
 why="French leaders wanted colonies, markets and prestige to rival Britain. The Red River promised a route into southern China.",
 then="French rule rested on forced labour on rubber plantations and heavy taxes and was resisted from the start. Vietnamese revolutionary Ho Chi Minh founded the Indochinese Communist Party in 1930.",
 fact="Hanoi's opera house, finished in 1911, was modelled on the Palais Garnier in Paris."),
"se-rizal": dict(
 bluf="Spanish colonial authorities executed Filipino writer José Rizal to make an example of the man they blamed for inspiring the Katipunan uprising. Rizal had argued for reform rather than revolution, and the firing squad in Manila on 30 December 1896 turned him into a martyr. The revolution spread, Filipino forces declared independence in 1898, and the Philippines gained a national hero whose weapon had been the pen.",
 now="Rizal's execution turned a reform movement into a revolution within two years and gave the Philippines a national hero whose method was writing rather than arms.",
 story="Filipino writer José Rizal's novels exposed the abuses of Spanish friars and officials. He advocated reform within the empire. When the Katipunan secret society launched an uprising in August 1896, Spain arrested him, tried him for rebellion and shot him in Manila on 30 December.",
 why="Spain blamed Rizal's writings for inspiring the Katipunan revolt and wanted to make an example of him.",
 then="The revolution spread. Filipino forces under Emilio Aguinaldo declared independence in June 1898, and the United States conquered the islands in the war that followed.",
 fact="Rizal, trained as an eye doctor, wrote his novels in Spanish; 'Noli Me Tangere' was printed in Berlin in 1887."),
"as189842": dict(
 bluf="The United States fought to hold the Philippines, bought from Spain for $20 million, as a Pacific base and gateway to Asian markets; Filipino forces under Emilio Aguinaldo fought for the independence they had declared. The US won after three years of war in which at least 200,000 Filipino civilians died, mostly of famine and disease. America became a colonial power in Asia, and the relationship it built produced the alliance that still ties the two countries.",
 now="Largely forgotten in the United States, the war is central to Philippine history; today's US-Philippine alliance, including bases facing China in the South China Sea, grew from this relationship.",
 story="After defeating Spain in 1898, the United States paid $20 million for the islands under the Treaty of Paris. Filipino forces under Emilio Aguinaldo, who had declared independence, fought the Americans from February 1899. The US declared the war over in July 1902. At least 200,000 Filipino civilians died, mostly from famine and disease.",
 why="The US wanted a Pacific base and Asian markets. US President William McKinley said Filipinos were unready to govern themselves.",
 then="US rule built schools and institutions and promised eventual self-government. The Philippines became a commonwealth in 1935 and independent in 1946.",
 fact="Aguinaldo was captured in March 1901 by American officers who entered his camp posing as prisoners of Filipino scouts in US service."),
"as194155": dict(
 bluf="Japan set out to seize the oil, rubber and tin of Southeast Asia after the US oil embargo of 1941 cut its supply. Its offensive of 8 December 1941 struck Malaya, Hong Kong and the Philippines; Singapore fell on 15 February 1942, the Dutch East Indies in March and the Philippines by May. Japan held the region until 1945, and the collapse of European authority made the return of the empires after the war far harder to sustain.",
 now="Four years of Japanese occupation, 1941 to 1945, broke the colonial administrations and armed the nationalists who fought the returning Dutch, French and British; most of the region was independent within twelve years of 1945.",
 story="The United States embargoed oil exports to Japan in August 1941. Japan's offensive of 8 December 1941 (7 December across the date line in Hawaii) struck Malaya, Hong Kong and the Philippines. Singapore fell on 15 February 1942, the Dutch East Indies in March and the Philippines by May.",
 why="Japan wanted the region's oil, rubber and tin after the US oil embargo.",
 then="Japan ruled the region until 1945, using forced labour on projects such as the Burma Railway. It armed local nationalists who later fought the returning Europeans.",
 fact="The Burma Railway killed more than 12,000 Allied prisoners and tens of thousands of Asian labourers in sixteen months of construction."),
"d-bataan": dict(
 bluf="Japan's army set out to move some 75,000 American and Filipino prisoners off Bataan after their surrender on 9 April 1942, with no plan for so many. It marched them about 100 km in the heat with little food or water and beat or killed those who fell; thousands of Filipinos and several hundred Americans died. News of the march, released in 1944, hardened American attitudes toward Japan, and it remains central to Philippine memory of the war.",
 now="The march made the treatment of prisoners an American war aim and gave the US-Philippine alliance a shared memory of suffering that both governments still invoke.",
 story="US and Filipino forces on Bataan surrendered on 9 April 1942 after months of siege. Japanese troops marched the prisoners about 100 km in the heat with little food or water. Those who fell were often beaten or killed. Thousands of Filipinos and several hundred Americans died on the march.",
 why="Japan was unprepared for so many prisoners. Its army treated surrender with contempt.",
 then="Thousands more died in the prison camps that followed. News of the march, released in 1944, hardened American attitudes toward Japan. Japanese General Masaharu Homma, who had commanded the invasion, was tried and executed in 1946.",
 fact="9 April is the Day of Valour, Araw ng Kagitingan, a national holiday in the Philippines."),
"d-singapore1942": dict(
 bluf="Japanese General Tomoyuki Yamashita set out to take Singapore, Britain's main base in Asia, by land down the Malay Peninsula while its guns faced the sea. On 15 February 1942 Lieutenant-General Arthur Percival surrendered about 80,000 British, Indian and Australian troops, the largest surrender in British military history. Belief in British power in Asia collapsed, and the empire never recovered its standing in the East.",
 now="The surrender ended the belief in British invincibility on which colonial rule in Asia rested; Malaya was independent within fifteen years and Singapore a state within 23.",
 story="Japanese General Tomoyuki Yamashita's army raced down the Malay Peninsula in two months and crossed the Johor Strait to Singapore. On 15 February 1942 Lieutenant-General Arthur Percival surrendered about 80,000 British, Indian and Australian troops. British Prime Minister Winston Churchill called it the worst disaster in British military history.",
 why="Singapore's defences faced the sea. Japan came by land with control of the air.",
 then="Many prisoners died building the Burma Railway. Japanese troops massacred tens of thousands of Chinese Singaporeans in the Sook Ching purge.",
 fact="Yamashita's roughly 30,000 men took the surrender of a garrison more than twice their size."),
"se-indonesia1945": dict(
 bluf="Indonesian nationalist leaders Sukarno and Mohammad Hatta set out to use Japan's defeat to end three and a half centuries of Dutch rule before the Dutch could return. Sukarno read a short proclamation of independence on 17 August 1945; four years of war followed before the Dutch, under US pressure, recognised independence in December 1949. The world's largest Muslim-majority nation emerged, and under Sukarno it became a leader of the non-aligned movement.",
 now="The proclamation fixed Indonesia's borders as those of the Dutch East Indies and made the army, forged in the war of 1945-49, the state's central institution, a position it held until 1998.",
 story="Japan's occupation had destroyed Dutch authority and promoted nationalist leaders. On 17 August 1945, two days after Japan's surrender, Indonesian nationalist leader Sukarno read a short proclamation of independence in Jakarta with Mohammad Hatta beside him. The Dutch returned and fought a four-year war to regain control. Under US pressure they recognised Indonesia's independence in December 1949.",
 why="Japan's defeat left a vacuum. Its occupation had destroyed Dutch authority and promoted the nationalist leaders who filled it.",
 then="Sukarno ruled until 1965-67. His 'Guided Democracy' gave way to General Suharto's military regime after the mass killings of 1965-66.",
 fact="The proclamation ran to two sentences."),
"se-vietnam1945": dict(
 bluf="Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh set out to seize power before France could return after Japan's defeat. The Viet Minh took control in the August Revolution, and on 2 September 1945 he declared independence in Hanoi, opening with 'All men are created equal' from the US Declaration of Independence. France returned with British help and war began in 1946; the fusion of communism with nationalism that Ho built fought France and then the United States for thirty years.",
 now="The declaration bound Vietnamese nationalism to the Communist Party, the combination that fought France to 1954 and the United States to 1975 and still governs Vietnam.",
 story="As Japan surrendered in August 1945, the Viet Minh took power in the August Revolution. On 2 September 1945 Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh read a declaration of independence in Ba Dinh Square, Hanoi. He opened with 'All men are created equal' from the US Declaration of Independence.",
 why="Japan's defeat left a vacuum. The Viet Minh were the best-organised nationalist force and moved before France could return.",
 then="France returned with British help. War with France began in 1946 and ran until the French defeat at Dien Bien Phu in 1954.",
 fact="Emperor Bao Dai abdicated in August 1945 and handed the imperial seal and sword to the Viet Minh."),
"se-philippines1946": dict(
 bluf="The United States set out to grant the Philippines independence on its own timetable, fixed by the Tydings-McDuffie Act of 1934, while keeping its bases and trade privileges. Independence came on 4 July 1946 under Philippine President Manuel Roxas, after a Japanese occupation that had devastated the country. US bases at Subic Bay and Clark remained until 1991-92 and trade terms limited Philippine economic policy for decades; the alliance remains central to US strategy in Asia.",
 now="The Mutual Defense Treaty of 1951 that followed independence makes the Philippines a US treaty ally; Manila invokes it in its disputes with China over the South China Sea, where the two states' claims overlap.",
 story="Under the 1934 Tydings-McDuffie Act, the Philippines became independent on 4 July 1946 after a ten-year transition and a Japanese occupation that devastated the country. Philippine President Manuel Roxas took office. The United States kept military bases and trade privileges.",
 why="Congress had promised independence in 1934 after a ten-year transition, partly because American farmers wanted to end duty-free Philippine imports.",
 then="US bases at Subic Bay and Clark remained until 1991-92. The Bell Trade Act of 1946 gave American firms parity rights and limited Philippine economic policy for decades.",
 fact="The Philippines moved its Independence Day from 4 July to 12 June, the date of the 1898 declaration, in 1962."),
"se-burma1948": dict(
 bluf="Burmese independence leader Aung San negotiated an end to British rule; he was assassinated with most of his cabinet in July 1947, six months before it came. Burma became independent on 4 January 1948 under Prime Minister U Nu, outside the Commonwealth, with its ethnic conflicts unresolved, and insurgencies began within months. The army, casting itself as guardian of national unity, took power in 1962 and has dominated the state since.",
 now="The ethnic settlement left open in 1948 is the war Myanmar is still fighting, and the army's claim to be the guardian of unity has justified its rule since 1962; Aung San's daughter, Aung San Suu Kyi, led the democracy movement against it.",
 story="Burmese independence leader Aung San negotiated independence with Britain. In July 1947 gunmen sent by a political rival assassinated him with most of his cabinet. Burma became independent on 4 January 1948 under Prime Minister U Nu, outside the Commonwealth. Communist and ethnic insurgencies began within months.",
 why="Wartime nationalism under Aung San gave Burma a movement Britain could neither crush nor ignore. A Britain exhausted by war and leaving India had no will to hold Burma.",
 then="The army under General Ne Win seized power in 1962 and has dominated politics since. Wars with ethnic minorities have continued for decades. The army's 2017 operations in Rakhine drove more than 700,000 Rohingya into Bangladesh (UN count, 2018); the army calls them a response to militant attacks, and UN investigators found genocidal intent.",
 fact="Independence was declared at 4.20 am on 4 January 1948, a time chosen by astrologers."),
"as195461": dict(
 bluf="French commanders built a base in a remote valley to lure the Viet Minh into a set-piece battle they expected to win with artillery and air power. Viet Minh General Vo Nguyen Giap hauled guns up the surrounding mountains by hand and surrounded the garrison, which surrendered on 7 May 1954 after 56 days. France agreed to leave Indochina at Geneva, Vietnam was divided, and an independence army had beaten a European power in conventional battle.",
 now="The defeat ended French rule in Indochina and partitioned Vietnam, which set up the American war; it showed every colonial army that it could lose a set-piece battle to an independence movement.",
 story="The French built a base at Dien Bien Phu, a remote valley near the Lao border, in late 1953. Viet Minh General Vo Nguyen Giap surrounded it with artillery hauled up the mountains by hand. After 56 days the French garrison surrendered on 7 May 1954.",
 why="The Viet Minh fought for independence from France. The French underestimated their artillery and chose a valley they could supply only by air.",
 then="France agreed to leave Indochina at the Geneva conference in July 1954, which divided Vietnam pending elections.",
 fact="The Viet Minh hauled artillery up the mountains by hand and bicycle, something the French thought impossible."),
"as195461b": dict(
 bluf="France wanted out of Indochina after Dien Bien Phu, and Britain, the Soviet Union, China and the United States wanted to avoid a wider war. At Geneva in July 1954 Vietnam was divided at the 17th parallel, with Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh's communists in the North and a French-backed state in the South, and nationwide elections were promised for 1956. The US-backed South refused to hold them, and the temporary line became the front of the Vietnam War.",
 now="Vietnam was reunified only by war, in 1975; the elections promised at Geneva never happened, a reminder that peace deals without enforcement often fail.",
 story="At Geneva, France agreed to leave Indochina. Vietnam was temporarily divided at the 17th parallel, with Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh's communists in the North and a French-backed state in the South. Nationwide elections were promised for 1956. The United States took note of the accords without signing them.",
 why="France had lost at Dien Bien Phu. The great powers wanted to avoid a wider war, and China pressed Ho to accept partition.",
 then="The US-backed South under Ngo Dinh Diem refused to hold the elections. A communist insurgency grew in the South from the late 1950s.",
 fact="Close to a million people, most of them Catholics, moved from North to South in the 300 days of free movement the accords allowed."),
"se-bandung": dict(
 bluf="Indonesian President Sukarno, Indian Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru, Chinese Premier Zhou Enlai and Egyptian leader Gamal Abdel Nasser gathered 29 Asian and African states to complete decolonisation and refuse a choice between the American and Soviet blocs. The conference condemned colonialism 'in all its manifestations' and called for peaceful coexistence. It founded the idea of a 'Third World' and led to the Non-Aligned Movement in 1961.",
 now="Bandung created the Afro-Asian bloc that drove the UN General Assembly's decolonisation agenda from 1960 and the Non-Aligned Movement of 1961; its vocabulary is the one the Global South still uses.",
 story="The Asian-African Conference met at Bandung in April 1955 with 29 countries representing over half the world's people. Indonesian President Sukarno hosted; Indian Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru, Chinese Premier Zhou Enlai and Egyptian leader Gamal Abdel Nasser led the main delegations. They condemned colonialism 'in all its manifestations' and called for peaceful coexistence and economic cooperation.",
 why="Newly independent states wanted to complete decolonisation. They refused to be forced to choose between the American and Soviet blocs.",
 then="It inspired the Non-Aligned Movement, founded at Belgrade in 1961, and African independence movements.",
 fact="A chartered plane carrying Chinese delegates to the conference was bombed in flight; Premier Zhou Enlai had changed his route and survived."),
"se-malaya1957": dict(
 bluf="Britain set out to hand Malaya to moderate leaders who would protect its rubber and tin interests while British forces fought a communist insurgency; Malayan leader Tunku Abdul Rahman set out to win independence by negotiation with an alliance of Malay, Chinese and Indian parties. Independence came on 31 August 1957. The model of Malay political dominance with ethnic power-sharing still defines Malaysian politics, and the Emergency ended in 1960.",
 now="The 1957 bargain, Malay political primacy with citizenship for Chinese and Indians, is the constitution Malaysia still governs under; its politics turn on relations between the ethnic communities.",
 story="Malayan leader Tunku Abdul Rahman's Alliance of Malay, Chinese and Indian parties won the elections of 1955. Britain granted independence on 31 August 1957 with the Tunku as prime minister. The Malayan Emergency against communist guerrillas, declared in 1948, lasted until 1960.",
 why="Britain wanted to hand power to moderate leaders who would protect its rubber and tin interests. An inter-ethnic alliance offered a stable partner while the communist insurgency was being defeated.",
 then="Malaya joined with Singapore, Sabah and Sarawak to form Malaysia in 1963. It expelled Singapore two years later amid ethnic tensions.",
 fact="The Tunku shouted 'Merdeka', freedom, seven times at the independence ceremony in the stadium built for it."),
"d-tonkin": dict(
 bluf="US President Lyndon Johnson wanted congressional authority to escalate in Vietnam. A clash on 2 August 1964 between the destroyer USS Maddox and North Vietnamese torpedo boats, and a reported second attack on 4 August, gave him the occasion, and Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution almost unanimously. No second attack had occurred; the resolution became the legal basis for an American war that peaked at over 500,000 troops.",
 now="The resolution gave the presidency a blank cheque that Congress repealed in 1971 and answered with the War Powers Resolution of 1973; declassified documents released in 2005 confirmed that the second attack never took place.",
 story="On 2 August 1964 North Vietnamese torpedo boats fought the USS Maddox, which was gathering intelligence off North Vietnam while US-backed South Vietnamese commandos raided the coast. A second attack was reported on 4 August. Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution on 7 August with two dissenting votes. Later evidence showed that no second attack had occurred.",
 why="The Maddox was gathering intelligence while commandos raided the coast. US President Lyndon Johnson wanted authority to escalate.",
 then="In 1965 Johnson sent combat troops and began sustained bombing. US troops in Vietnam peaked at over 500,000 in 1968.",
 fact="Senators Wayne Morse and Ernest Gruening cast the only two votes against the resolution."),
"na196578b": dict(
 bluf="US President Lyndon Johnson set out to stop South Vietnam falling to communism, which he feared would spread across Asia. With the war powers of the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution he began bombing North Vietnam in February 1965 and landed Marines at Da Nang in March; by 1968 more than 500,000 US troops were in Vietnam. The United States withdrew in 1973 without securing the South, and the war divided the country, undermined trust in government and reshaped US foreign policy for a generation.",
 now="Vietnam left lasting American distrust of foreign interventions and of official claims about wars.",
 story="The Gulf of Tonkin Resolution of August 1964 gave US President Lyndon Johnson broad war powers. The US began sustained bombing of North Vietnam in February 1965 and landed Marines at Da Nang in March. By 1968 more than 500,000 US troops were in Vietnam. Washington described the war as the defence of an ally against invasion from the North; Hanoi described it as a war of national liberation against American aggression.",
 why="Johnson feared that if South Vietnam fell, communism would spread across Asia, the 'domino theory'. He also feared the political cost at home of losing a country to communism.",
 then="Rising casualties and the 1968 Tet Offensive turned many Americans against the war. The last US combat troops left in 1973 and Saigon fell in 1975. More than 58,000 Americans died; Vietnam's government put Vietnamese deaths at about 3 million in a 1995 estimate, and other estimates run from 1 to 2 million.",
 fact="Operation Rolling Thunder, the bombing of the North begun in March 1965, ran for more than three years."),
"se-indonesia1965": dict(
 bluf="Indonesia's army under Major General Suharto set out to destroy the Communist Party (PKI), the world's third largest, after an attempted coup on 30 September 1965 that the army blamed on the party. Over the following months the army and allied militias killed an estimated 500,000 to 1 million suspected communists and imprisoned hundreds of thousands more. Suharto pushed President Sukarno aside and ruled for 31 years with Western backing; no one has been prosecuted for the killings.",
 now="The killings founded Suharto's New Order, which ruled from 1966 to 1998 on anti-communism and the army's claim to guard the state; the ban on the PKI and on communist ideas remains law, and no one has been prosecuted.",
 story="On the night of 30 September 1965 a group calling itself the 30 September Movement killed six army generals in Jakarta. The army under Major General Suharto blamed the Communist Party (PKI). Over the following months the army and allied civilian groups killed an estimated 500,000 to 1 million people in Java, Bali, Sumatra and elsewhere. Hundreds of thousands more were imprisoned without trial. Estimates of the dead range from 78,000, the figure of a commission President Sukarno appointed in early 1966, to the 500,000 to 1 million most widely cited since.",
 why="Cold War fears came first. Rivalry between the army and the PKI gave the army its target, and local religious and social conflicts gave the killings their footsoldiers. One reading holds that the PKI planned the coup and that the killings were a popular response the army channelled; the other holds that the army organised and directed the killings and that the PKI leadership's role in the 30 September Movement remains unproven.",
 then="Suharto pushed Sukarno aside in March 1966 and ruled for 31 years with Western support. He built an economy open to foreign investment and an army-dominated state.",
 fact="The PKI had about 3 million members in 1965, the largest communist party outside the Soviet Union and China."),
"se-singapore1965": dict(
 bluf="Malaysian Prime Minister Tunku Abdul Rahman set out to end two years of race riots and political clashes with Lee Kuan Yew's mostly Chinese People's Action Party by expelling Singapore from the federation. Singapore became independent on 9 August 1965, a city-state with no natural resources and a prime minister who wept as he announced it. Under Lee it became one of the world's richest countries through trade, foreign investment and an efficient, authoritarian government.",
 now="Singapore's expulsion produced a city-state that from 1965 built prosperity on trade, foreign capital and one-party rule, the model of a small state prospering through openness and strong government that others cite.",
 story="Singapore had joined Malaysia in 1963. Two years of race riots and political clashes between Singaporean leader Lee Kuan Yew's People's Action Party and the Malay-led federal government followed. Malaysian Prime Minister Tunku Abdul Rahman had Singapore expelled, and it became independent on 9 August 1965. Lee wept on television as he announced the separation.",
 why="Lee's mostly Chinese party campaigned for a 'Malaysian Malaysia' with equal rights for all communities. The Malay-led government in Kuala Lumpur saw this as a threat to Malay primacy.",
 then="Under Lee, who governed until 1990, Singapore became one of the world's richest countries per person.",
 fact="Singapore is often described as the only country to have gained independence against its own wish."),
"se-asean": dict(
 bluf="Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand set out to manage their own disputes after the Indonesia-Malaysia confrontation and to stand together against communist expansion. Their foreign ministers signed the ASEAN Declaration in Bangkok in August 1967. The body grew to ten members by 1999 and eleven in 2025, and it became the forum through which Southeast Asia balances between the United States and China.",
 now="ASEAN's members have about 680 million people, and the bloc tries to balance between the United States and China.",
 story="The foreign ministers of Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore and Thailand signed the ASEAN Declaration in Bangkok on 8 August 1967. It aimed to promote regional cooperation and stability, partly as a bulwark against communism.",
 why="Ending the Indonesia-Malaysia confrontation of 1963-66 came first. Fear of communist expansion as the Vietnam War grew gave the five a shared interest.",
 then="Brunei joined in 1984. Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar and Cambodia joined in the 1990s, and Timor-Leste became the eleventh member in 2025.",
 fact="The founding declaration ran to two pages; the charter that gave ASEAN a legal personality came into force only in 2008."),
"d-mylai": dict(
 bluf="US soldiers of Charlie Company set out to destroy a village suspected of sheltering the Viet Cong, under orders and after weeks of casualties from mines and snipers. On 16 March 1968 they killed between 347 and 504 unarmed villagers at Son My. The army covered it up until journalist Seymour Hersh exposed it in 1969; the revelation deepened American opposition to the war, and only one officer was convicted.",
 now="The massacre and its cover-up, exposed in November 1969, deepened American opposition to the war and made the duty to refuse unlawful orders a fixed part of military training.",
 story="On 16 March 1968 soldiers of US Army Charlie Company searching for the Viet Cong killed between 347 and 504 villagers of Son My; the lower figure is the US Army's count, the higher the figure on the Vietnamese memorial. Helicopter pilot Hugh Thompson landed between soldiers and villagers to stop the killing. The army covered it up until journalist Seymour Hersh exposed it in November 1969.",
 why="Frustration over casualties from mines and snipers came first. Poor leadership and orders to destroy a village suspected of sheltering the Viet Cong turned a sweep into killing.",
 then="Only Lieutenant William Calley was convicted. He served three and a half years, mostly under house arrest, after US President Richard Nixon ordered him released from the stockade.",
 fact="Hugh Thompson received the Soldier's Medal for his actions in 1998, thirty years after the massacre."),
"d-tet": dict(
 bluf="North Vietnam's leaders set out to break the South Vietnamese government with surprise attacks across the South during the Lunar New Year truce, hoping to spark a popular uprising. About 80,000 troops struck more than 100 towns and cities at the end of January 1968 and were beaten back with heavy losses, though they held Hue for a month. The uprising never came; the fighting convinced many Americans the war was unwinnable, and US President Lyndon Johnson halted most bombing, declined to run again and opened talks.",
 now="Tet turned American opinion from 1968 onward, making the war one to exit rather than win; every later government has feared the gap between official optimism and televised fact that Tet exposed.",
 story="During the Lunar New Year truce at the end of January 1968, about 80,000 North Vietnamese and Viet Cong troops attacked more than 100 towns and cities, including the grounds of the US embassy in Saigon. US and South Vietnamese forces beat them back with heavy losses. Hue was held for a month. Americans who had been told victory was near were shocked.",
 why="Hanoi hoped surprise attacks across the South would spark a popular uprising and break the South Vietnamese government.",
 then="US President Lyndon Johnson halted most bombing of the North on 31 March 1968 and announced he would not run again. Peace talks began in Paris in May.",
 myth="Tet was a North Vietnamese battlefield victory. It was a military defeat for the North, with the Viet Cong's southern cadres largely destroyed; the political effect in the United States was the victory.",
 fact="Viet Cong sappers blew a hole in the wall of the US embassy compound in Saigon and held the grounds for six hours."),
"na197382": dict(
 bluf="US President Richard Nixon set out to withdraw from a war that had become deeply unpopular while claiming 'peace with honour'. The Paris Peace Accords of January 1973 took the last US combat troops out by March and returned American prisoners of war; more than 58,000 Americans had died. Fighting between North and South continued, Saigon fell in April 1975, and the United States emerged with a lasting reluctance to fight long foreign wars.",
 now="The 'Vietnam syndrome' kept the United States out of large ground wars from 1973 to the Gulf War of 1991 and shaped the all-volunteer army that replaced the draft the same year.",
 story="US envoy Henry Kissinger and North Vietnamese negotiator Le Duc Tho reached terms in Paris. The accords, signed on 27 January 1973, led to the withdrawal of the last US combat troops in March and the return of American prisoners of war. More than 58,000 Americans had died. Fighting between North and South went on.",
 why="The war had become deeply unpopular at home. Nixon sought 'peace with honour'.",
 then="Congress cut American aid to Saigon. Without US troops or air support, South Vietnam fell to a North Vietnamese offensive in April 1975.",
 fact="Kissinger and Le Duc Tho were awarded the 1973 Nobel Peace Prize; Tho refused it."),
"as197566": dict(
 bluf="North Vietnam's leaders set out to finish the war by conventional offensive once US troops and aid were gone. Their spring offensive collapsed the South's army in weeks, and tanks entered Saigon on 30 April 1975 as helicopters lifted the last Americans out. Thirty years of war ended with Vietnam unified under the Communist Party; the United States avoided ground wars for fifteen years, and the predicted 'domino' collapse stopped at Indochina.",
 now="The fall of Saigon ended thirty years of war and American intervention in Southeast Asia, reunified Vietnam under the Communist Party, and left a United States that avoided ground wars for fifteen years.",
 story="North Vietnam's spring offensive of March 1975 collapsed the South's army in weeks. As helicopters evacuated the last Americans from the US embassy, North Vietnamese tanks entered Saigon on 30 April 1975. South Vietnamese President Duong Van Minh surrendered. The city was renamed Ho Chi Minh City.",
 why="South Vietnam depended on US support, which had ended. Corruption undermined its state, and the North was determined.",
 then="Communists also took power in Laos and Cambodia in 1975. Vietnam's re-education camps and economic failure drove hundreds of thousands of 'boat people' to flee over the next decade.",
 fact="The United States and Vietnam restored diplomatic relations in 1995, twenty years after the fall."),
"as197566b": dict(
 bluf="Pol Pot's Khmer Rouge set out to build an agrarian communist society from nothing. After taking Phnom Penh on 17 April 1975 they emptied the cities, abolished money, schools and religion, and killed or starved roughly a quarter of Cambodia's people by 1979. Vietnam's invasion ended the regime in January 1979; a UN-backed tribunal convicted the surviving leaders of genocide in 2018, and the toll remains the century's most intense in proportion to population.",
 now="The Khmer Rouge killed about a quarter of Cambodia's people in four years, the most intense genocide of the century in proportion to population; Vietnam's invasion to end it isolated both countries for a decade.",
 story="On 17 April 1975 the Khmer Rouge under Pol Pot took Phnom Penh and forced its entire population into the countryside. They abolished money, schools and religion. Roughly a quarter of Cambodia's people were killed or died of starvation and overwork by 1979. Estimates of the dead run from 1.5 to 2 million, the range the UN-backed tribunal worked with, to 3.3 million, the figure of the Vietnamese-installed government of the 1980s.",
 why="The spread of the Vietnam War into Cambodia helped the Khmer Rouge grow, and the US-backed government of Lon Nol collapsed. One reading holds that American bombing of 1969-73 drove peasants to the Khmer Rouge; the other holds that the party's growth came from North Vietnamese backing and from Prince Sihanouk's call to join it after the 1970 coup against him. Pol Pot's aim of total agrarian revolution set the scale of the killing.",
 then="Vietnam invaded in December 1978 and drove the Khmer Rouge from power in January 1979. China, the United States and others kept recognising the Khmer Rouge at the UN for years.",
 fact="A UN-backed tribunal convicted the surviving leaders of genocide in 2018."),
"se-cambodia1978": dict(
 bluf="Vietnam set out to end Khmer Rouge raids across its border and to remove a hostile regime backed by its rival China. It invaded on 25 December 1978 and took Phnom Penh in January 1979, ending the Khmer Rouge genocide. China attacked Vietnam in February 1979 in a short war, Vietnam occupied Cambodia until 1989, and Cold War alignments kept the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia's UN seat for years.",
 now="The invasion ended the genocide and installed the government in which Hun Sen rose, prime minister from 1985 to 2023; the Chinese-Vietnamese hostility it sealed shaped Indochina until the Paris agreement of 1991.",
 story="Khmer Rouge forces raided across the Vietnamese border through 1977 and 1978. Vietnam invaded on 25 December 1978 and took Phnom Penh on 7 January 1979. China, the Khmer Rouge's backer, attacked Vietnam's northern border in February 1979 in a short, bloody war.",
 why="Khmer Rouge raids across the border came first. Vietnam's rivalry with China, the Khmer Rouge's backer, made removal of the regime a strategic aim.",
 then="Vietnam occupied Cambodia until 1989. Hanoi called the invasion a liberation; China, ASEAN and the United States treated it as aggression and an occupation, and the Khmer Rouge kept Cambodia's UN seat, from 1982 as part of a coalition, until the 1991 peace agreement.",
 fact="Hun Sen, a Khmer Rouge commander who defected to Vietnam in 1977, became prime minister in 1985 at the age of 32."),
"se-peoplepower": dict(
 bluf="Philippine opposition leader Corazon Aquino and the Catholic Church under Cardinal Jaime Sin set out to remove Philippine President Ferdinand Marcos after he claimed victory in a fraudulent election in February 1986. When military rebels broke away, about two million people protected them along EDSA avenue for four days; Marcos fled to Hawaii and Aquino became president. The peaceful revolution inspired movements in South Korea and Eastern Europe, and a new constitution limited presidents to a single term.",
 now="The 1987 constitution written after the revolution still governs the Philippines; Marcos's son, Ferdinand Marcos Jr., won the presidency in 2022 with a majority of the vote.",
 story="Philippine President Ferdinand Marcos claimed victory in a fraudulent election in February 1986. Defence Minister Juan Ponce Enrile and General Fidel Ramos broke away on 22 February. Cardinal Jaime Sin called people to the streets, and about two million protected the rebels along EDSA avenue. Marcos fled to Hawaii on 25 February, and Corazon Aquino became president.",
 why="Twenty years of Marcos rule, martial law and corruption came first. The 1983 assassination of opposition leader Benigno Aquino made his widow the opposition's candidate.",
 then="A new constitution in 1987 limited presidents to a single six-year term. The example inspired protest movements in South Korea in 1987 and Eastern Europe in 1989.",
 fact="Imelda Marcos left more than a thousand pairs of shoes behind in the Malacañang Palace."),
"as199772b": dict(
 bluf="Thailand set out to defend a baht pegged to the dollar against speculators and gave up on 2 July 1997. Foreign money fled, currencies and stock markets crashed in Indonesia, South Korea and Malaysia, and IMF bailouts came with harsh conditions. Indonesian President Suharto fell in 1998, South Korea restructured its conglomerates, Malaysia imposed capital controls, and Asian states built foreign reserves so as never to depend on the IMF again.",
 now="East Asian states built foreign reserves after 1997 to avoid ever again needing the IMF; the Chiang Mai currency-swap network of 2000 and China's reserve accumulation grew from the same lesson.",
 story="Thailand floated the baht on 2 July 1997 after failing to defend its dollar peg. Foreign money fled. Currencies and stock markets crashed in Indonesia, South Korea and Malaysia. IMF bailouts for Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea came with harsh conditions.",
 why="Fixed exchange rates came first. Short-term foreign borrowing and property bubbles left banks and companies exposed when the pegs broke.",
 then="Indonesian President Suharto resigned in May 1998 amid riots. South Korea restructured its giant conglomerates, and Malaysia imposed capital controls in September 1998.",
 fact="The Indonesian rupiah lost more than three-quarters of its value against the dollar within a year."),
"se-timor": dict(
 bluf="East Timor's independence movement under Xanana Gusmão and José Ramos-Horta set out to end an Indonesian occupation begun in 1975, in which an estimated 100,000 to 180,000 people died. A UN-run vote in 1999 chose independence, pro-Indonesian militias destroyed much of the territory, and a UN administration brought independence on 20 May 2002. The first new state of the century was also the first the United Nations itself administered into existence.",
 now="Independence ended 24 years of Indonesian occupation that had killed up to a quarter of the population, and it was the first new state the United Nations itself administered into existence.",
 story="Indonesia invaded the former Portuguese colony in December 1975. Perhaps 100,000 to 180,000 Timorese died during the occupation, the range of Timor-Leste's truth commission in 2005. After Indonesian President Suharto fell in 1998, a UN-run vote in August 1999 chose independence by 78.5 per cent, and pro-Indonesian militias destroyed much of the territory in revenge. A UN administration led to independence on 20 May 2002 with resistance leader Xanana Gusmão as president.",
 why="Indonesia's democratisation after Suharto's fall in 1998 came first. Indonesian President B.J. Habibie's surprise offer of a vote in January 1999 opened the way.",
 then="UN peacekeepers stayed until 2012. Timor-Leste remains one of Asia's poorest and most democratic states.",
 fact="Timor-Leste joined ASEAN in 2025. Ramos-Horta shared the 1996 Nobel Peace Prize with Bishop Carlos Belo."),
"se-tsunami": dict(
 bluf="A magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra on 26 December 2004 sent waves up to 30 metres high onto coasts from Indonesia to East Africa, and the Indian Ocean had no warning system to alert anyone. About 230,000 people died in 14 countries, Aceh worst of all. The disaster pushed Aceh's rebels and Indonesia's government into a peace deal in 2005 and produced an Indian Ocean tsunami warning system.",
 now="The Helsinki agreement of 2005 that ended Aceh's three-decade insurgency grew directly from the disaster, the model case of a catastrophe opening space for peace; the Indian Ocean warning system built from 2005 covers coasts that had none.",
 story="On 26 December 2004 a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra sent waves up to 30 metres high onto coasts from Indonesia to Thailand, Sri Lanka, India and East Africa. About 230,000 people died in 14 countries. Aceh, nearest the epicentre, was the worst hit, and more than half the dead were Indonesian.",
 why="An undersea earthquake of magnitude 9.1 off Sumatra. The Indian Ocean had no tsunami warning system, so coastal populations had no warning.",
 then="Aceh's rebels and Indonesia's government signed a peace deal at Helsinki in August 2005. An Indian Ocean warning system was built.",
 fact="The fault rupture lasted some eight to ten minutes, the longest ever recorded by instruments."),
"se-myanmar2021": dict(
 bluf="Myanmar's army under Senior General Min Aung Hlaing set out to reverse the November 2020 election, which Aung San Suu Kyi's party had won by a landslide and which the army said was fraudulent. On 1 February 2021 it arrested her and her government and crushed mass protests. Armed resistance spread into a nationwide civil war in which resistance forces and ethnic armies hold large parts of the country, the most serious challenge the army has faced since 1948.",
 now="The war has displaced more than 3 million people (UN count, 2024); the junta's phased elections that began in December 2025 are, in the army's account, a return to constitutional rule and, in the account of the opposition and most Western governments, a device to legitimise the coup.",
 story="Aung San Suu Kyi's National League for Democracy won the November 2020 election by a landslide. The army claimed fraud; the election commission and foreign observers rejected the claim. On 1 February 2021 the army under Senior General Min Aung Hlaing arrested Suu Kyi, President Win Myint and other leaders and declared a state of emergency. Security forces shot protesters in the streets, and armed resistance spread across the country.",
 why="The army refused to accept further loss of power to the civilian government. Its case rests on alleged fraud in the 2020 vote; the opposition's case rests on the election commission's and foreign observers' finding that the vote was credible.",
 then="Resistance forces under a National Unity Government and ethnic armies took large parts of the country from 2023. The army, backed by China and Russia, answered with air strikes on villages. Civilians killed by security forces passed 6,000 by 2025 in the count of a Burmese rights group.",
 fact="Aung San Suu Kyi, 75 at the time of the coup, was sentenced in closed trials to a total of 33 years, later reduced to 27."),
}
